  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-3{t}^{2}+1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e815{t}^{2}-4t,\\,4{t}^{3}-6t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-6t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e830t-4,\\,12{t}^{2}-6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =\u27e830(2)-4,\\,12(2)^{2}-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e860-4,\\,48-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e856,\\,42\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(2)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(2)| & =|\u27e856,\\,42\u27e9| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{56}^{2}+{42}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3136+1764} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4900} \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is $70{\\text{\u00a0m/s}}^{2}.$",
